     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. A confirmed dose doesn't change the safety problem. 5,100 mg/day of metformin is twice the maximum daily dose, and a 1,700 mg single dose is well above the per-dose limit. The risks are lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, and the family visit doesn't change them. I also can't see a clinical reason for exceeding the labeled maximum, since metformin gives no added glycemic benefit above about 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- **Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals** (2,000 mg/day)\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO twice daily with meals** (1,700 mg/day)\n\nIf you need glycemic control beyond these doses, I'd suggest adding a second agent or insulin rather than raising the metformin dose. A pharmacy consult could help you settle the regimen before evening rounds.\n\nIf you do have a clinical rationale for the higher dose, it should go through your institution's pharmacy or override process, not through me."
      }
